WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.330
Validation is where threat hunting becomes disciplined analysis.

2
00:00:04.330 --> 00:00:12.915
Not every unusual event is suspicious, not every suspicious event is malicious, and not every malicious-looking event is confirmed.

3
00:00:12.915 --> 00:00:21.271
These words should mean different things in notes and reports. Unusual means the activity differs from a baseline or expectation.

4
00:00:21.271 --> 00:00:27.348
Suspicious means the activity has characteristics that could align with adversary behavior.

5
00:00:27.348 --> 00:00:31.983
Malicious means there is evidence of harmful or unauthorized activity.

6
00:00:31.983 --> 00:00:41.403
Confirmed means the evidence has reached the threshold your organization uses for incident handling. A validation workflow starts with context.

7
00:00:41.403 --> 00:00:50.975
Compare the event against baselines. Has this user authenticated from this location before? Does this server normally run administrative scripts?

8
00:00:50.975 --> 00:00:57.356
Is the process common for that software package? Is the destination domain part of a vendor service?

9
00:00:57.356 --> 00:01:05.332
Baselines are not perfect, but they help identify what requires deeper review. Next, check asset role and identity context.

10
00:01:05.332 --> 00:01:11.334
A command that is normal on an administrator jump host may be unusual on a finance workstation.

11
00:01:11.334 --> 00:01:20.526
A service account authenticating at night may be expected if it runs a scheduled job, but suspicious if it appears on endpoints it never touches.

12
00:01:20.526 --> 00:01:30.250
A developer workstation may generate traffic that would be odd for a point-of-sale system. Context prevents flat rules from becoming false positives.

13
00:01:30.250 --> 00:01:31.769
Review related events.

14
00:01:31.769 --> 00:01:43.620
A single process event may look odd, but the parent process, command line, user, network connection, file write, and surrounding authentication events can change the interpretation.

15
00:01:43.620 --> 00:01:51.369
If a DNS request is unusual, identify the clients, timing, proxy activity, and endpoint process context when available.

16
00:01:51.369 --> 00:02:01.169
If a privileged logon is unusual, review the source, target, authentication type, and nearby administrative actions. Look for business explanations.

17
00:02:01.169 --> 00:02:10.589
Maintenance windows, software deployment tools, vulnerability scanners, backup jobs, and help desk activity can all produce strange patterns.

18
00:02:10.589 --> 00:02:19.553
Validating benign explanations does not weaken the hunt. It improves the conclusion and may produce useful allow lists or tuning guidance.

19
00:02:19.553 --> 00:02:22.060
Preserve evidence before escalation.

20
00:02:22.060 --> 00:02:31.404
Save relevant event identifiers, query outputs, timestamps, affected assets, user context, screenshots if appropriate, and the queries used.

21
00:02:31.404 --> 00:02:35.507
Do not alter systems or collect data beyond your authorization.

22
00:02:35.507 --> 00:02:45.914
If evidence suggests active compromise, credential misuse, unauthorized access, or material risk, escalate to incident response through the defined process.

23
00:02:45.914 --> 00:02:52.295
Careful language matters. Say what the evidence shows, what it suggests, and what remains unknown.

24
00:02:52.295 --> 00:02:58.829
That discipline protects the credibility of the hunt and helps responders prioritize the right work.

25
00:02:58.829 --> 00:03:03.235
False positives are not failures when they teach the team something.

26
00:03:03.235 --> 00:03:12.883
A false positive can reveal a legitimate administrative workflow, a missing asset tag, a noisy service account, or a detection that needs enrichment.

27
00:03:12.883 --> 00:03:20.024
Record that learning. The next hunt or alert rule can use the context to reduce noise without losing visibility.
